@moduledoc """
The behaviour of a connector: one kind of source, and how what it sends becomes
records. Add a connector to `config <%= inspect(@app) %>, <%= inspect(@integrations) %>, connectors: [...]`.

`transports/0` says how data arrives, and which callbacks are implemented:

  * `:file` - `accepts?/2` (is this dropped file mine?) and `parse_file/3`,
  * `:webhook` - `handle_webhook/2`, given the request's headers, raw body and
    decoded JSON. Check the caller first: `authorized?/2` checks the source's
    bearer token, and the raw body is there for a provider's own signature,
  * `:api` - `sync/1`, run every few minutes for each active source
    (`source.credentials` is loaded; `source.cursor` is what the last sync
    returned).

A connector whose credentials come from a browser handshake (Plaid Link, OAuth)
implements `setup_path/0`, the page where a source of it is set up: the form at
`/integrations` leaves it out and links there, and a source of it is refused
without credentials. A connector that holds something elsewhere (an access
token, a provider's Item) implements `disconnect/1`: it is called, with the
credentials loaded, before the source is deleted, and an error stops the delete.
`handle_webhook/2` may be implemented without `:webhook` in `transports/0`, for a
provider's own notifications that the connector verifies itself: no bearer token
is issued and no token button is shown.

Every callback returns records: `type` and `external_id` identify one within its
source (a record is stored once; deliver it again and it is a duplicate),
`data` is what the application's `<%= inspect(@integrations) %>.Handler` reads. A record
is never updated in place: a change to something already delivered needs a new
`external_id`.

`{:reject, message}` means "this will never work" (the input is malformed);
`{:error, reason}` means "try again later".
"""

@type entry :: %{
        required(:type) => String.t(),
        required(:external_id) => String.t(),
        required(:data) => map(),
        optional(:occurred_at) => DateTime.t() | nil
      }

@doc "Stable identifier, stored on sources."
@callback key() :: String.t()
@callback name() :: String.t()
@callback description() :: String.t()
@callback transports() :: [:file | :webhook | :api]

@callback accepts?(upload :: map(), body :: binary()) :: boolean()

@callback parse_file(source :: map(), upload :: map(), body :: binary()) ::
            {:ok, [entry()]} | {:reject, String.t()} | {:error, term()}

@callback handle_webhook(source :: map(), %{
            headers: [{String.t(), String.t()}],
            raw_body: binary(),
            params: map()
          }) ::
            {:ok, [entry()]}
            | {:reject, String.t()}
            | {:error, :unauthorized}
            | {:error, term()}

@callback sync(source :: map()) :: {:ok, [entry()], cursor :: map()} | {:error, term()}

@doc "Where a source of this connector is set up when the generic form cannot (see the module doc)."
@callback setup_path() :: String.t()

@doc "Revokes what the connector holds elsewhere for `source` (its credentials are loaded) before the source is deleted."
@callback disconnect(source :: map()) :: :ok | {:error, term()}

@optional_callbacks accepts?: 2,
                    parse_file: 3,
                    handle_webhook: 2,
                    sync: 1,
                    setup_path: 0,
                    disconnect: 1

@doc """
Whether the request carries `source`'s webhook token as
`authorization: Bearer <token>`. Only the token's hash is stored, and it is
compared in constant time.
"""
@spec authorized?(map(), [{String.t(), String.t()}]) :: boolean()
def authorized?(%{webhook_token_hash: hash}, headers) when is_binary(hash) do
  case List.keyfind(headers, "authorization", 0) do
    {_name, "Bearer " <> token} when token != "" ->
      Plug.Crypto.secure_compare(hash_token(token), hash)

    _missing ->
      false
  end
end

def authorized?(_source, _headers), do: false

@doc false
@spec hash_token(String.t()) :: String.t()
def hash_token(token), do: :crypto.hash(:sha256, token) |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)
